{"kind":"assert","text":"ARC-1 (3c6e6296): the measurement is accepted. An admitted entry at the 128-use limit never archives in one pass, so the confirmed sequence stays behind it and the archive retries without end. I rule on the remedy from numbers, not yet.\nWrite a short design amendment (notes only, checker review) that prices two remedies side by side:\nA. The staged copy you propose: retained progress per source scope and entry hash, resumed across bounded passes.\nB. One rule at admission: the scope admits only an entry that one archive pass can copy under the existing ceilings. No new archive state.\nFor each, state: the storage rows added and their bound; the wake rate while an entry is unconfirmed, as the floor and as requests per room per hour, with the backoff; what a restart, a moved entry and a partial result leave behind and who removes it; and which shipped or example definition has an act near the limit, with its use count. For B, give the largest use count that one pass copies, measured, and name any existing act it would refuse.\nAlso state the present cost: with the 128-use entry stuck, how many archive requests per hour does that room make today?\nNo production source before the ruling. H2 (4145ef57) is read; it stays behind O2, E2 and E3.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}